[
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm taking a look at Mathlib's <code>backward.privateInPublic true</code>'s, and noticing that a fair number of them can be resolved simply by putting <code>by exact</code> in the right place: a public type involves supplying some (term-mode) proof within the type, that proof uses a private declaration, and elaboration fails with \"unknown identifier; this declaration is private but would need to be public\". Wrapping the whole proof in <code>by exact</code> then fixes things.</p>\n<p>This is because <code>by</code> blocks abstract the proof term they produce out into an aux theorem if possible, and so can switch to <code>isExporting := false</code> when elaborating the syntax (which is now effectively the body of the aux theorem).</p>\n<p>But <code>by exact</code> is quite an unusual solution to \"Unknown identifier; a private declaration exists but would have to be public\"! :)</p>\n<p>To give an example:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">module</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">private</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">my_thm</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">expose</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">public</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bool</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/--</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">error: Unknown identifier `my_thm`</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">Note: A private declaration `my_thm` (from the current module) exists but would need to be public to access here.</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">-/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"n\">public</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">my_thm</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">public</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">my_thm</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>There's <code>as_aux_lemma</code> in tactics, and hypothetically there could be a term elaborator...but this too would be an awkward solution. Is there a better approach for this?</p>\n<p>Note that there are also sometimes private definitions that create data which are <em>used</em> inside a proof within a public position. So simply testing to see if the expected type at the \"unknown identifier\" error is a prop is insufficient.</p>\n<p>For other reasons, I've wondered if it would make sense for term elaboration to \"know\" when it was inside a proof: <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"270676\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/Pre-RFC.3A.20Synthing.20noncomputable.20instances.20in.20proofs.3F/with/558755382\">#lean4 &gt; Pre-RFC: Synthing noncomputable instances in proofs?</a> I now wonder if that same approach could have other benefits, and could solve this problem...though I can imagine that this is not a complete approach (what happens if there are metavariables in the expected type? Do you postpone elaboration on \"unknown identifier\" when a private one exists, and wait and see if this winds up being a proof?). Still, maybe it could be made to work.</p>\n<p>In the meantime, a linter could likely detect and suggest something helpful. But it would be nice to fix this \"at the source\" if possible. :)</p>",
        "id": 614890950,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Murrills",
        "timestamp": 1785984769
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe <code>by exact</code> should be spelt <code>private</code> to match</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">expose</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">public</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">fst</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">private</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">snd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">private</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">print</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 614903895,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1785992554
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That's definitely an option. What is much harder is to apply it automatically for proofs, even just doing so for structure fields invariably breaks stuff in Mathlib because the elaboration ordering is affected (basically you need to elaborate the public fields before the private ones): <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/13751\">lean4#13751</a></p>",
        "id": 614930002,
        "sender_full_name": "Sebastian Ullrich",
        "timestamp": 1786004029
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oof, but thanks for testing :)</p>\n<p>Though, interestingly, I chose maybe three of the errors, and hand-wrote <code>by exact</code> in front of all of the fields, and they succeeded. Would it make any sense to simply create a by-style pending mvar for the proof field, and just rely on <code>runTactic</code> being called whenever/however it gets called? Or, otherwise somehow avoid forcing <code>synthesizeSyntheticMVarsNoPostponing</code> before we're ready.</p>",
        "id": 615050736,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Murrills",
        "timestamp": 1786040785
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm not sure I'm following. Implementing the postponement isn't a problem, the issue is that it is a change in elaboration ordering and thus an observable change in semantics. It doesn't happen often but that's often enough at Mathlib scale</p>",
        "id": 615053477,
        "sender_full_name": "Sebastian Ullrich",
        "timestamp": 1786041817
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>IOW, it breaks whenever Mathlib relies on a proof value to infer mvars in the proof type</p>",
        "id": 615053805,
        "sender_full_name": "Sebastian Ullrich",
        "timestamp": 1786041945
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think my point is that if the change in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/13751\">lean4#13751</a> <em>did</em> postpone the elaboration (currently it doesn't, right?), it might not change the elaboration order in a breaking way.</p>",
        "id": 615054087,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Murrills",
        "timestamp": 1786042048
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Either way I'm trying to figure out why that PR breaks proofs that succeed under a manual <code>by exact</code>.</p>",
        "id": 615054184,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Murrills",
        "timestamp": 1786042087
    },
    {
